{
  "case_id": "2002-02-258",
  "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nROBERT S. PECK, ESQ., Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nJEFFREY A. LAMKEN, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the United States, as Intervenor.\nANDREW F. LINDEMANN, ESQ., Columbia, South Carolina; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In enacting section 1367, Congress took up this Court's invitation in Finley to manage the boundaries of supplemental jurisdiction. It had two goals in doing so. It sought to provide a Federal forum for plaintiffs that so chose to use it, and for -- for reasons of respect for the interests of comity and federalism, it provided a mechanism by which those cases may be returned to State court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, the Anti-Injunction Act just applies to Federal courts, doesn't it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: I didn't understand it that way, I thought South Carolina said, Federal court, you want to take this and deal with this stale claim? That's all right with us."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0000-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The other -- Justice Souter, the other possible consequence is that plaintiffs, fearful that a Federal court will not hear their matter, will not take it back if the -- the State courts will not accept the matter, may be left without a cause of action on their State claim, that they will suddenly be shut out the door."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lindemann",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "By enacting section 1367(d), Congress has intruded on principles of State sovereignty."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: What about examples cited by the representative of the Solicitor General of the Soldiers' and Sailors' Civil Relief Act and other Federal laws that have a similar effect on South Carolina and other States?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lindemann",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I would -- I would submit that there is no difference in the Soldiers' and Sailors' context, but there would be a major difference in any of the situations involving a stay."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lindemann",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I would submit to Your Honor that that is certainly not a very substantial Federal interest to the extent it is a Federal interest."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lindemann",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That's why we are not pursuing this matter under the Eleventh Amendment. However, a municipality has standing to assert a challenge under the Tenth Amendment, and this Court in the Printz case, Printz v. United States, was"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Was that -- was that a -- an immunity --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lindemann",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "reason why we believe that this is a significant issue under the Tenth Amendment for this case and why the Eleventh Amendment jurisprudence of this Court does not govern is -- is because of the importance of the State law interest."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Thank you, Mr. Lindemann."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Peck",
      "case_id": "2002-02-258",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "74f75e6d7dbe31d69121ac5609c55f84728c284c43323567a1fb8e9790b336ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-74f75e6d7dbe-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If the Court has no further questions, I would ask that the Supreme Court of South Carolina be reversed and would waive rebuttal."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
